{
 "id": "2026-ho-how",
 "name": "How Does This Street Make You Feel? Taipei street-view walks and 16 emotions",
 "description": "Method replication: same task, scales and design as the paper; the videos are not the paper's stimuli (14 new walk-through videos made from consecutive Google Street View panoramas in Taipei, captured 2024–2025, © Google). Based on Ho, Wei, Li & Chiang, \"Revealing emotional responses to urban environmental elements through street view data and deep learning\" (Environment and Planning B: Urban Analytics and City Science 53(3), 647–672; doi:10.1177/23998083251348280). Each participant watches one street-view walk, drawn at random, to the end, imagines being on that street, and rates 16 emotions from Russell and Pratt's circumplex model on a 1–7 scale (1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree), with the paper's wording \"I feel … in this environment\": aroused, astonished, happy, delighted, satisfied, content, serene, relaxed, frustrated, afraid, alarmed, angry, sad, tired, bored, depressed. Then gender, age (20 or younger, 21–30, 31–40, 41 or older), education, place of residence and familiarity with Taipei, the paper's covariates. The original study had 2,090 online participants (recruited on Facebook), 50 sampling areas of 500 m × 500 m in five kinds of area (business, residential, educational, parks and green space, water), and related the ratings to street-view elements found by deep learning.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 14 videos of about 30 seconds (each about 45 panoramas along 300–450 m of street, joined with cross-fades) instead of 50 videos of 3–4 minutes recorded by navigating every street of each area; (2) the walks start in business (3), residential (3), educational (3), park (3) and waterside (2) areas, and the file name gives the area where the walk starts, but a walk follows the street and can pass into other kinds of area; a car-based waterside walk shows little water; (3) no practice video before the task; (4) the page lets the participant continue only after the video has played to the end, and records the seconds watched.",
 "author": "Ho, Wei, Li & Chiang",
 "year": "2026",
